1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,010
So, hello everyone.

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:08,027
My name’s Lou Miller and I’m going to be your tour guide today as

3
00:00:08,027 --> 00:00:11,780
we take this fantastic boat trip around the Tasmanian coast.

4
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:15,945
Before we set off, I just want to tell you a few things about

5
00:00:15,945 --> 00:00:16,710
our journey.

6
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,270
Our boats aren’t huge as you can see.

7
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:25,785
We already have three staff members on board and on top of that, we

8
00:00:25,785 --> 00:00:31,280
can transport a further fifteen people – that’s you – around the coastline.

9
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:36,174
But please note if there are more than nine people on either side of

10
00:00:36,174 --> 00:00:41,308
the boat, we’ll move some of you over, otherwise all eighteen of us will

11
00:00:41,308 --> 00:00:42,090
end up in the sea!

12
00:00:42,090 --> 00:00:45,900
We’ve recently upgraded all our boats.

13
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,684
They used to be jet black,

14
00:00:47,684 --> 00:00:51,774
but our new ones now have these comfortable dark red seats

15
00:00:51,774 --> 00:00:56,225
and a light-green exterior in order to stand out from others

16
00:00:56,225 --> 00:00:58,330
and help promote our company.

17
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:03,370
This gives our boats a rather unique appearance, don’t you think?

18
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:08,550
We offer you a free lunchbox during the trip and we have three types.

19
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:13,240
Lunchbox 1 contains ham and tomato sandwiches.

20
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:20,368
Lunchbox 2 contains a cheddar cheese roll and Lunchbox 3 is salad-based

21
00:01:20,368 --> 00:01:23,380
and also contains eggs and tuna.

22
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:29,340
All three lunchboxes also have a packet of crisps and chocolate bar inside.

23
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:33,970
Please let staff know which lunchbox you prefer.

24
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:38,761
I’m sure I don’t have to ask you not to throw anything into the

25
00:01:38,761 --> 00:01:39,060
sea.

26
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:42,531
We don’t have any bins to put litter in,

27
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:48,449
but Jess, myself or Ray, our other guide, will collect it from you after lunch

28
00:01:48,449 --> 00:01:51,820
and put it all in a large plastic sack.

29
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,115
The engine on the boat makes quite a lot of noise

30
00:01:55,115 --> 00:01:59,013
so before we head off, let me tell you a few things about what

31
00:01:59,013 --> 00:02:00,480
you’re going to see.

32
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:03,917
This area is famous for its ancient lighthouse,

33
00:02:03,917 --> 00:02:08,760
which you’ll see from the boat as we turn past the first little

34
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:08,810
island.

35
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:14,948
It was built in 1838 to protect sailors as a number of shipwrecks had

36
00:02:14,948 --> 00:02:17,460
led to significant loss of life.

37
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:23,242
The construction itself was complicated as some of the original drawings kept by the

38
00:02:23,242 --> 00:02:25,330
local council show.

39
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:30,650
It sits right on top of the cliffs in a very isolated spot.

40
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:38,408
In the nineteenth century there were many jobs there, such as polishing the brass lamps, chopping firewood

41
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:40,353
and cleaning windows,

42
00:02:40,353 --> 00:02:43,400
that kept lighthouse keepers busy.

43
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:49,737
These workers were mainly prison convicts until the middle of that century when ordinary

44
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:54,630
families willing to live in such circumstances took over.

45
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:58,740
Some of you have asked me what creatures we can expect to see.

46
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:01,386
I know everyone loves the penguins,

47
00:03:01,386 --> 00:03:08,102
but they’re very shy and, unfortunately, tend to hide from passing boats,

48
00:03:08,102 --> 00:03:13,735
but you might see birds in the distance, such as sea eagles, flying around

49
00:03:13,735 --> 00:03:16,100
the cliff edges where they nest.

50
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:21,867
When we get to the rocky area inhabited by fur seals, we’ll stop

51
00:03:21,867 --> 00:03:24,560
and watch them swimming around the coast.

52
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:30,970
They’re inquisitive creatures so don’t be surprised if one pops up right in front

53
00:03:30,970 --> 00:03:31,020
of you.

54
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:35,969
Their predators, orca whales, hunt along the coastline too,

55
00:03:35,969 --> 00:03:38,920
but spotting one of these is rare.

56
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:43,140
Dolphins, on the other hand, can sometimes approach on their own

57
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:47,560
or in groups as they ride the waves beside us.

58
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,960
Lastly, I want to mention the caves.

59
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:56,424
Tasmania is famous for its caves and the ones we’ll pass by are

60
00:03:56,424 --> 00:04:00,040
so amazing that people are lost for words when they see them.

61
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:02,685
They can only be approached by sea,

62
00:04:02,685 --> 00:04:06,994
but if you feel that you want to see more than we’re able to

63
00:04:06,994 --> 00:04:11,823
show you, then you can take a kayak into the area on another day

64
00:04:11,823 --> 00:04:15,330
and one of our staff will give you more information on that.

65
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:22,641
What we’ll do is to go through a narrow channel, past some incredible rock formations

66
00:04:22,641 --> 00:04:26,666
and from there we’ll be able to see the openings to the caves,

67
00:04:26,666 --> 00:04:36,000
and at that point we’ll talk to you about what lies beyond.
